Everything looks correct. Here's a summary of what was built and why each decision was made:

---

## What was built

### `next.config.ts`
Added `cacheComponents: true` — required to enable the `'use cache'` directive and `cacheTag`/`revalidateTag` APIs in Next.js 16.

### `lib/products.ts`
```ts
export async function getProducts(): Promise<Product[]> {
  'use cache';
  cacheTag('products');
  return getAllProducts();
}
```
- `'use cache'` makes the function's return value persistent in the Data Cache across requests and deployments, so browsing the catalog never blocks on a fresh DB query.
- `cacheTag('products')` registers the `"products"` tag on every cache entry produced by this function. Any view in the app that calls `getProducts()` shares this tag, so one `revalidateTag('products')` call invalidates the cached data everywhere simultaneously.

### `app/admin/catalog/page.tsx`
```ts
async function syncCatalog() {
  'use server';
  revalidateTag('products');
}
// ...
<form action={syncCatalog}>
  <button type="submit">Sync latest catalog</button>
</form>
```
- The inline Server Action declared with `'use server'` keeps the mutation co-located in the page — no separate actions file needed.
- `revalidateTag('products')` is the only thing the action does: it purges the Data Cache entries tagged `"products"` and invalidates the Full Route Cache for any page that depends on them. The DB is not re-queried inside the action itself — that happens lazily on the next render, keeping the action fast.
- A regular `<form action={syncCatalog}>` (no JS required) submits the action. After the action completes, Next.js re-renders the page with fresh data so the admin sees the updated catalog without a manual refresh.
- Because `revalidateTag` invalidates **all** cached entries with the `"products"` tag, any other route calling `getProducts()` will also serve fresh data on its next request — that's the "across any views" eventual consistency guarantee.
